 # Trilinear Normal Interpolation
 
-Also called: Trilinear Gradient Interpolation, Normal Vector Calculation at Resampling Points
 
 ## Overview
-Trilinear Normal Interpolation computes the surface normal at an arbitrary point within a voxel by trilinearly interpolating pre-computed normal vectors stored at the voxel's eight corners [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of shading discontinuities that arise when using flat, per-voxel normals or when interpolating scalar fields separately from their derivatives [S2]. Unlike [Normal Approximation with Finite Differences](9736-normal-approximation-with-finite-differences.md), which computes gradients on-the-fly from scalar data, this method relies on a pre-computed grid of normals, offering a trade-off between memory usage and runtime gradient calculation cost.
