 ### Normal Vector Calculation at Resampling Points
-This variant, described in [S2], specifically applies trilinear normal interpolation to resampling points in volume rendering to improve shading accuracy. It differs by explicitly deriving the corner normals from the gradients of scalar amplitude data $F$ using central finite differences before interpolation. It is chosen when the volume data is scalar and smooth, and high-quality lighting is required without storing a separate normal grid. The process involves normalizing the 8 corner gradients, interpolating them, and normalizing the result again [S2].
+This variant applies trilinear normal interpolation to resampling points in volume rendering [S2]. It calculates the normal vector by interpolating the normalized gradient vectors of the eight surrounding voxel points [S2]. The process normalizes each of the eight gradient vectors, performs trilinear interpolation on them, and normalizes the resulting vector [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per point, involving a fixed number of arithmetic operations (8 multiplications/additions for weights and vector sum) [S1, unofficial][S2].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Depends on storage. If normals are pre-computed and stored, it requires $3 \times N$ floats for $N$ grid points [S1, unofficial]. If gradients are computed on-the-fly from scalar data, it requires access to the scalar grid and temporary storage for 8 gradient vectors [S2].
-- **Strengths:** Provides smooth, continuous normals across voxel boundaries, reducing shading artifacts [S2]. Faster than computing gradients on-the-fly if the normal grid is already available [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Requires pre-computation and storage of normals if not derived from scalar data [S1, unofficial]. Can suffer from precision issues if the interpolated vector is very close to zero (degenerate case) [G].
-- **When to use:** When high-quality shading is needed and pre-computed normals are available or gradients are cheap to compute.
-- **When not to use:** When memory is extremely constrained and normals are not pre-computed, or when the scalar field is noisy (finite differences may amplify noise) [S2].
